WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
Prompts are code, so treat them like code.

2
00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:05.656
A prompt that runs in production is part of your software.

3
00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:12.592
It changes behavior when it changes, it can break things, and someone will need to know why it is worded the way it is.

4
00:00:12.872 --> 00:00:29.725
OpenAI’s prompting guide now says this plainly: keep production prompts in your application code so they get typed inputs, code review, tests, and your normal deployment process, and add representative fixtures and evaluation checks before changing a production prompt.

5
00:00:30.005 --> 00:00:42.912
The same guide recommends pinning production apps to specific model snapshots and building tests that measure prompt behavior, because different snapshots, even within one model family, can produce different results.

6
00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:45.240
In practice that means a few habits.

7
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.296
Keep each prompt in version control, next to the code that calls it.

8
00:00:49.576 --> 00:00:56.893
Give each published version a name (v1, v2) and treat it as immutable: to change it, add a new version and compare.

9
00:00:57.173 --> 00:01:04.043
Record the model and settings the prompt was tested with, since a prompt plus a model is the unit that works or doesn’t.

10
00:01:04.323 --> 00:01:12.771
And write down why each change was made, in the commit message or a short change log, so the next person doesn’t undo a fix they don’t understand.

11
00:01:13.051 --> 00:01:21.691
As a PM, you already know this pattern from requirements: a spec has versions, changes go through review, and every change has a reason.

12
00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:27.133
The difference here is that you can also run the spec against tests, automatically, in seconds.
